@host = http://localhost:5100
@serviceClientId = keyward-demo-service
@serviceClientSecret = ChangeMe!Service-Secret
@interactiveClientId = keyward-demo-spa

### The discovery document. Everything a client library needs to configure itself.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/openid-configuration

### The public keys. A relying party validates access tokens against these and never calls back here.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks

### A machine-to-machine token. Note what does not come back: no refresh token, deliberately.
POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id={{serviceClientId}}&client_secret={{serviceClientSecret}}&scope=api

### A scope this client was never granted. Registered, but not for it.
POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id={{serviceClientId}}&client_secret={{serviceClientSecret}}&scope=api reports

###
# The interactive flow starts in a browser, because it has to: there is a login form, possibly a
# second-factor challenge, and a consent screen. Open the URL below, sign in as user@keyward.local with
# the password ChangeMe!User1, and read the code off the address bar you are redirected to.
#
# The code_challenge below is the S256 hash of this verifier:
#   dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk
###
GET {{host}}/connect/authorize
  ?client_id={{interactiveClientId}}
  &response_type=code
  &redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A5199%2Fcallback
  &scope=openid%20email%20profile%20roles%20offline_access%20api
  &code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM
  &code_challenge_method=S256
  &state=state-value
  &nonce=nonce-value

### Exchange the code. Paste the one from the redirect into @code first.
@code = paste-the-code-here

POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code&client_id={{interactiveClientId}}&code={{code}}&code_verifier=dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A5199%2Fcallback

### Rotate. The response carries a different refresh token, and this one is now spent.
@refreshToken = paste-the-refresh-token-here

POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=refresh_token&client_id={{interactiveClientId}}&refresh_token={{refreshToken}}

###
# Send the request above a second time, with the same (now spent) refresh token.
#
# It fails with invalid_grant, which is unsurprising. What matters is what happens next: send the
# request with the *new* refresh token you received a moment ago, and that fails too. Replaying one
# token in a chain kills the chain. Then look at the audit trail below.
###

### Who the token says you are.
@accessToken = paste-the-access-token-here

GET {{host}}/connect/userinfo
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### The sessions an account has. Needs a token carrying the admin role.
@userId = paste-the-sub-claim-here
@operatorToken = paste-an-admin-access-token-here

GET {{host}}/admin/users/{{userId}}/sessions
Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}

### End one session by the handle the listing above gives you.
@familyId = paste-a-session-id-here

POST {{host}}/admin/sessions/{{familyId}}/revoke
Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}

### End every session for an account. The stolen-laptop call.
POST {{host}}/admin/users/{{userId}}/sessions/revoke
Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}

### The authentication trail, newest first.
GET {{host}}/admin/audit?limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}

### Just the replays. This should return nothing, forever.
GET {{host}}/admin/audit?type=RefreshReuseDetected
Authorization: Bearer {{operatorToken}}

### Liveness.
GET {{host}}/health/live

### Readiness, which includes the database.
GET {{host}}/health/ready
